  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 242 PM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\n(Saturday Night - Tuesday)\n\nWe will quickly transition from a cool and rainy pattern for some to \nheat and humidity by the end of the weekend as the mid/upper level \nridge amplifies and shifts eastward. Temperatures at 850 hPa climb \nclose to +25C, or near the 99th percentile of climatology. \nConfidence is increasing in widespread 100-105+ heat index values \neach day beginning on Sunday lasting through Tuesday. Magnitude heat \nadvisory criteria will probably be reached across portions of the \narea on one or multiple days, but we are far enough out still to \nhold off on the issuance of heat products. The other potential fly \nin the ointment is a low threat of showers/thunderstorms moving in \nfrom the north. There is very little signal in deterministic model \nguidance attm, but the mid/upper level anticyclone itself does stay \nto our west. In addition, forward-propagating Corfidi vectors are \nmostly northerly, so IF a complex gets organized to our north it \ncould move into our region. Depending on the timing, this could \nknock down the heat on any given day, but also would threaten us \nwith damaging wind gusts.\n\n\n(Tuesday Night - Next Thursday)\n\nForecast uncertainty begins to increase by the middle of next week \nas ensemble guidance is in pretty good agreement that the anomalous \nmid/upper level ridge will weaken slightly and shift back to the \nsouthwest. This should at least increase the threat of a backdoor \ncold front and/or a ridge-running shortwave to produce \nshowers/thunderstorms. Either of these scenarios would at least \nslightly cool temperatures and take the edge off of the heat. \nHowever, there are no strong signs through at least next Thursday of \na strong air mass change. Therefore, in the absence of a backdoor \ncold frontal passage or showers/thunderstorms, it is possible the \nheat/humidity will continue past Tuesday.",
